WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:11.091
"Please answer me, sir!" the Persian said, keeping pace with Raoul, who was hurrying away from the managers'

2
00:00:11.092 --> 00:00:15.913
office. "Where are you going in such a rush?" "To Christine's assistance, sir," Raoul replied without slowing down.

3
00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:24.885
"Mifroid, the police commissioner, said my brother has abducted her, and no one knows where he's taking her.

4
00:00:24.886 --> 00:00:30.747
I must get my carriage and pursue them." "If you want to rescue Christine, you'll find her in

5
00:00:30.748 --> 00:00:37.259
the opera house," the Persian said. Raoul stopped and turned to him in surprise. "You mean she's here

6
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:46.909
with Erik?" The Persian nodded. Raoul was skeptical of this man he'd only just met. His eyes narrowed

7
00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:56.472
as he demanded, "How do you know this?" "I was at the performance tonight, and only Erik could

8
00:00:57.384 --> 00:01:03.241
have conceived of an abduction like that, snatching Christine right from the stage." The Persian sighed. "I recognized

9
00:01:03.242 --> 00:01:14.332
the monster's touch." "So you're acquainted with Erik?" Raoul said. The Persian sighed again. "Yes, I first encountered

10
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:22.891
him in my role as chief of police in my country." "Sir," Raoul said, "I don't know what

11
00:01:22.892 --> 00:01:26.476
your intentions are, but can you do anything to help me, to help Christine?" "I can attempt to

12
00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:38.742
take you to her . . . and to him." "If you can do that, I'll be eternally

13
00:01:38.743 --> 00:01:48.849
grateful, but I must ask one question," Raoul said, pausing for a moment. "Do you think it's possible

14
00:01:48.849 --> 00:01:58.116
that my brother abducted Christine?" "No," the Persian said, shaking his head. "From what I've heard about your

15
00:01:59.327 --> 00:02:09.501
brother, he isn't capable of a kidnapping, never mind a dramatic one." Raoul nodded in agreement. He had noticed that the Persian, unlike Mifroid, hadn't laughed at the mention of Erik. "I believe you know Erik

16
00:02:09.502 --> 00:02:19.617
is real and a threat to Christine," Raoul said. "So I place myself in your hands—take me to

17
00:02:19.618 --> 00:02:24.110
Christine and Erik." "Silence!" The Persian appeared to be listening to distant sounds in the opera house. "We

18
00:02:24.111 --> 00:02:31.544
must avoid using his name in case he overhears us." Raoul looked around in alarm. "Do you think

19
00:02:31.545 --> 00:02:45.417
he's nearby?" "I don't know, but it's quite possible he's here in this wall or floor or ceiling."

20
00:02:45.418 --> 00:02:49.979
The Persian led Raoul through passages that Raoul had never seen before. After traveling up and down several

21
00:02:49.979 --> 00:03:00.070
staircases, they stopped at a door, which the Persian opened with a master key. Across from the door

22
00:03:00.734 --> 00:03:07.637
was the entrance to Christine's dressing room. Raoul was impressed—he hadn't realized this route to her dressing room

23
00:03:07.638 --> 00:03:20.272
even existed. "You know the opera house so well, sir." "Not half as well as he does," the

24
00:03:20.273 --> 00:03:26.960
Persian muttered as they entered Christine's room and shut the door. The Persian pressed his ear against one

25
00:03:26.961 --> 00:03:40.955
wall and coughed softly. There was the sound of a person stirring, and seconds later, someone tapped on

26
00:03:40.956 --> 00:03:45.391
the door. "I've summoned my servant, Darius, who was waiting in the storage room next door," the Persian

27
00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:55.340
explained. A man wearing a long overcoat entered the room. He bowed, took a richly carved case from

28
00:03:55.341 --> 00:04:01.951
under his coat, placed it on Christine's dressing table, bowed again, and went to the door. "Darius, did

29
00:04:02.345 --> 00:04:09.112
anyone see you come in here?" the Persian asked. "No." "Then don't let anyone see you leave." Darius

30
00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:17.649
opened the door, glanced up and down the passage, and quickly disappeared. The Persian opened the case, which

31
00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:28.094
contained two pistols. "When Christine was abducted, I sent word to Darius to bring these." He handed a pistol to Raoul. "We'll be fighting the most terrifying enemy you can imagine, but you must do it

32
00:04:28.095 --> 00:04:38.556
for Christine's sake. You love her, don't you?" "I worship the ground she walks on!" Raoul declared. "But you don't even know Christine, so why are you ready to risk your life for her? You must

33
00:04:38.556 --> 00:04:48.512
hate Erik." "No, I don't hate him," the Persian said, shaking his head sadly. "I've forgiven him for

34
00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:51.489
the harm he's done to me, but that's a story for another time." "You say he's a monster,"

35
00:04:52.093 --> 00:04:59.140
Raoul said, "yet I detect in you the same pity for him that Christine has. Her pity nearly

36
00:04:59.141 --> 00:05:05.336
drove me to despair." The Persian did not reply. Instead he fetched a stool and set it in

37
00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:13.035
front of the wall opposite the large mirror. He climbed onto the stool and seemed to be looking

38
00:05:13.036 --> 00:05:22.593
for something in the pattern of the wallpaper. At last he tapped a spot. "There it is—in half

39
00:05:22.594 --> 00:05:29.907
a minute, we'll be on our way to his house." The Persian turned around and jumped off the

40
00:05:29.908 --> 00:05:35.607
stool. He crossed the room and pressed his hand against the mirror. "It's not moving yet." "Are we

41
00:05:35.607 --> 00:05:45.757
going through the mirror?" Raoul asked. "I saw Christine do that on the night of the masked ball."

42
00:05:46.468 --> 00:05:52.353
"And what did you think of that?" "I thought I was seeing things!" Raoul said. "Or that it

43
00:05:52.354 --> 00:05:59.690
was some new trick of the opera ghost's." The Persian laughed as he pressed his hand on the

44
00:05:59.691 --> 00:06:04.894
mirror again. "Oh, if only we were dealing with a ghost, things would be much simpler. We must

45
00:06:04.895 --> 00:06:13.067
make ourselves as invisible as possible. Cover the front of your white shirt as much as you can

46
00:06:13.068 --> 00:06:17.566
with your coat." Raoul followed the Persian’s instructions. "Why isn't the mirror opening?" "It doesn't open; it turns,"

47
00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:20.865
the Persian said. "When you press the button in this room, the mechanism takes time to work. But

48
00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:29.267
when he's behind the wall, he can make the mirror turn instantly." "That's probably what happened on the

49
00:06:29.268 --> 00:06:35.550
night of the masked ball." "If you watch, you'll see the mirror rise a few inches and then

50
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:45.473
shift a little from left to right. It will then be on a pivot and swing around." "The mirror still isn't doing anything!" Raoul was impatient to get going. "It will—unless, of course, he's disabled the

51
00:06:45.474 --> 00:06:51.866
mechanism because he knows we're coming." "And if he's done that, what about Christine?" "We'll do all that's

52
00:06:51.867 --> 00:07:00.601
humanly possible to save her, although he may stop us before we reach her. He commands the walls,

53
00:07:00.602 --> 00:07:07.563
the doors, and the trapdoors. In my country, he was known as the man who loves trapdoors." "But

54
00:07:07.564 --> 00:07:14.855
why do the walls and doors obey him?" Raoul asked, puzzled. "He didn't build them." "Actually, he did

55
00:07:14.856 --> 00:07:20.819
build them! And his house lies within the walls of the opera house cellars." Before Raoul could ask

56
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:27.723
another question, the mirror rose and shifted sideways. As the Persian pulled Raoul close to him, the mirror

57
00:07:27.724 --> 00:07:34.054
turned like a shimmering revolving door. The two men were hurled from the dressing room into total darkness.

